WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:05.600
- We are back.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.030
I'm here with Kaheer Suleman,
who is the co-founder and CTO of Maluuba,

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:14.520
and who is now a principal
research program manager at Microsoft.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.170
And Jianfeng Gao, a research manager
at Microsoft Research Redmond.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.290
They're going to talk to us today
about literate machines.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:23.250
They will share with us a little bit
about the latest research in getting machines

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.210
to read with comprehension
and write like people do.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:27.780
Welcome Kaheer and Jianfeng.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:28.420
How are you doing?

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.190
- Happy to be here.
- Great.

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:30.910
Happy to be here as well.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.740
- So I've studied MLP,
I've studied machine translation.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:37.070
I don't know what machine reading comprehension is,

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:40.230
so why don't you start out
with describing what it actually is?

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.030
That way we can get a sense for what it is.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:49.770
- So the groups here at Microsoft,
whether it's Maluuba or Redmond or Microsoft Asia,

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:55.170
we're working on this vision
of having neural networks

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:58.970
be able to take in large amounts of unstructured text

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:01.360
and then be able
to perform tasks with it.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.080
And these tasks can be something
like question answering,

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.530
so we want to be able to really
comprehend the information that is there.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:13.040
And you can almost break down reading comprehension
into these multiple levels of skills,

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.580
one being reasoning,

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.680
the other being knowledge representation.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.400
How do we represent knowledge
about the world?

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.360
How do we represent
knowledge in the text?

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.720
And then finally extending
that beyond to common sense.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:27.870
What is, how do we know
about everyday things in our lives?

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:32.420
And how do we then take that from text
and do something useful with it?

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:33.760
- I see.
Jianfeng?

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.960
- I can give you a bit of history.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:45.620
Over the last 20 years we have tried to pass
large amounts of test data on web and dictionaries.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:51.600
And at any point, entities and their relationships
to create the knowledge graph, as Kaheer said.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.320
- Sure.
- But recently we hit a wall

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.470
in terms of performance,
by approaching it this way.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.560
And do you know what the missing piece?

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.890
- No.
I mean, I remember doing something,

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.340
for example like word net,

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.870
and deep mind was like
an extension of word net

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:07.270
that sort of mapped all these entities.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.280
But I always felt like, how are you
going to represent everything?

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.920
- That's one of the key issues.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.750
And actually, when you have the critical
missing piece, the so-called semantic reasoning.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.270
Put it this way.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:28.240
If there wasn't an exact text stream
that matched the idea, you could not find the answer.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.060
- I see.
- Quite often there are tons

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.770
of different ways of saying
how long a river is.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:41.240
And remember, there's a key difference
between similar words and similar concepts.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:47.850
That's why we resolve to neural networks
to relate the semantically similar concepts

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:51.950
and entities by mapping them
into a hidden semantic space

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.090
where semantically the data,

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.690
the concepts are paired together.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:01.540
- So my question is, I understand
how deep mind would stop working

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:03.580
because it's looking
for like regular text,

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.240
and it doesn't find the exact text.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.570
Or if the question is phrased
in a way it has never seen,

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:09.530
it's never going to be able
to pull the semantic meaning.

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:13.700
But my question to you is, that model
I understand how to code it, right.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.950
I understand how to work it.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:20.590
How are you using deep learning
to represent text without its task?

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:23.540
Because generally when we have text,
there is like a task.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:28.400
I want you to find, I remember the first MLP thing is,
we have a bunch of emails from a disease outbreak,

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.000
and I need to find what the disease was and what died.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.290
It was a specific task.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:36.590
But with machine reading comprehension,
there's really no task, right?

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:41.950
- Well I mean the task, so if we're talking
about understanding word meanings,

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:45.040
we create this task based on this idea

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:46.490
that words are known,

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:50.260
their meaning is told by its neighbors,
so the words that are around it.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:54.930
So we build these models that can predict words
around it, so that we can get word meanings.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.690
But there is a task with reading comprehension.

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:57.910
- OK.
- It's a QA task.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:01.470
So how do we test child comprehension?

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:05.700
We give them a passage, we give them questions,
and then we say answer them based on this passage.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.190
And that's how we've been setting
up tasks for reading comprehension.

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:15.530
- So the main target or the main output of this
is we want it to consume all this text,

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:19.620
and we want to be able to ask it anything
about anything in any particular form.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.460
Is that right?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:26.680
- And so how does it deal
with ambiguous questions?

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:28.350
That's the thing I have a hard time with.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:33.480
For example, Dr. Grosz was talking about hey,
where are the 10 nearest pharmacies?

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:34.930
Which one is open?

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:36.520
How does it deal with context?

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:37.900
Can it do that?

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.900
Tell me about that word.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:45.230
- This has something related
to the hidden semantic space.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:53.730
In that space, all the language of different
explanations are what we represent using vectors.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.740
- OK.
- In that hidden space.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.080
That's the interesting thing
about this space.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.660
If there's two test streams,
they are semantically similar,

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.689
so no matter how they are different from surface form

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:10.240
they will stick together
in that semantic space.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:11.760
And that's the magic.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.850
- And so this network is literally gluing
all of these things together in a way

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:19.830
that we maybe don't quite understand,
but seems to work.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:25.340
- And that's why in most cases the most effective model
is trained by performing tasks,

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.510
not by remembering things.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:29.360
- I see.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:30.460
And that's a human feature.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:33.460
So where is the state of the art
in this area right now?

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:36.420
Let's talk about, like, is it working?

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:40.150
- So, I mean, I think the biggest
and most interesting areas

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:43.160
that have seen improvement
recently are in the area of reasoning.

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:48.340
The ability to use something called
an attention model to figure out,

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:51.430
to learn, to look where
in the text semantically things

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:52.850
that are related to the question,

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:55.850
be able to combine these facts
that are in the text together.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:06:00.810
Also, you know, we've made some progress
looking at QA in a different angle

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:02.550
as a generation task.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:06.760
So where you model reasoning not
from the retrieval point of view,

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:09.570
where we are just answering a question,

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.880
you look at it from, how do we,

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.190
what's the mechanism
for generating the question

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:15.760
that led to the answer?

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.330
- Oh, I see.
- So these are kind of the areas

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:20.650
that I think have really seen
the biggest improvement.

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:25.270
But of course, I think some of the other areas
like knowledge representation and common sense,

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:26.620
there's still work to go.

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:27.210
- What about you?

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.810
Where is the state of the art for this?

00:06:29.503 --> 00:06:33.990
- I agree with Kaheer, I can give you an example.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:38.770
Recently we've developed a neural natural
reading comprehension called the Read

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.380
Net which has shown
a lot of promise.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:48.560
The model uses the neural networks
to not only remember things,

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.080
but learn to perform real-world tasks

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:54.330
using knowledge such as question answering.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:07:00.200
Actually, although we don't know
exactly how the human brain works,

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:05.180
this is a constant source
of inspiration for us.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:10.180
For example, Read Net tries to mimic
the inference process of human readers.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:11.800
Think about this.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.940
When you read a document,
there's a question mark.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:19.620
You always read a documents several times,
each time focusing on different portions

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:20.920
of the document

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:25.116
until a satisfied
answer is formed in your brain.

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:28.860
Our Read Network is just like that,
but in the neural space.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:33.790
- So maybe, like I just barely started
understanding neural networks.

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:38.350
I remember learning neural networks,
but now that they're a lot deeper,

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:40.720
they can do a lot more,
I'm trying to understand,

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:43.360
how does it encode
that information?

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:46.450
- That's through training.

00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:49.810
For example,
we use large amounts

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:51.590
of question-answer pairs

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:52.790
as a training aid,

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:56.480
to help the Read Net
to learn the models.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.780
I'll give you an example.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:02.690
If, for example, the model,
you give the model a question.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:06.090
The model will try to form
an answer to the question

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:08.600
by leveraging the useful information

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:10.870
or relevant information
stored in its memory.

00:08:11.510 --> 00:08:14.500
But in the case that such information
is missing in memory,

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:19.343
then the Read Net needs to learn to seek

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:21.370
and to store new information

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:23.240
by updating its memory

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:25.450
via the so-called
reinforcement memory.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:31.130
So that neural network will be able
to answer the question next time.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:35.590
- So for training, if I'm understanding
this right, you give it a lot of questions.

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:37.740
It creates a representation.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:40.160
And then you ask it more questions
and more questions

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:41.590
and then it updates
its representation.

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:42.070
- Yes.

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:43.530
- What does that representation look like?

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:53.100
- So one of the things there's been some interesting
work is on visualizing these representations.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.420
So if you could almost look at it like,
the way we have to look at it is the way

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.690
we visualize things
going on in the brain.

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:03.800
We have to, because what it really is,
is a large vector space

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:07.270
where certain things
get activated based on certain words.

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:11.630
That's what's happening, right, so you can look at it
like the way people look at FMRI.

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:17.990
So when certain words appear,
certain neurons get activated in the neural network,

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:20.360
just like what's
happening in the brain.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:24.660
Certain regions of the brain get lit up,
and that's sort of what it really looks like.

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:28.230
And it could look different
at any different time of the network.

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:29.420
- I see.

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:31.430
What do these vectors look like?

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:37.050
I mean, when I'm asking a question, like I'm trying,
because I'm a programmer at heart.

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.490
When it comes down to it,
these theories are very nice.

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:41.240
But then we've got to type
something into a computer.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:44.950
Like when it's coming in, what kind
of transformations are you doing

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:48.530
and then how do you actually get the thing
to spit out a reasonable answer?

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:51.550
Because it's not just a question
of getting information out,

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:53.960
but then it's got to write it out
in a way that a human can understand it.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:55.990
- Yes.
So what happens is,

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:58.100
and Jianfeng can talk more
in detail about this,

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:01.080
is you start off from what we call
the symbolic space,

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:03.380
which is the words.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.870
They get transformed
into a set of vectors.

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:10.290
These vectors then get transformed over
and over again by the neural network

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:12.780
that's doing the reasoning
as it updates to memory.

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:18.960
Then at the end, we transform it back by
making predictions based on the output words.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:20.730
So we transform it back
to the symbolic space.

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:24.220
So now your answer is in the space
that you and I understand.

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:30.120
Internally, you know, these vectors
and different regions of this vector space

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:32.360
are going to get activated
when different words come in.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:33.250
- I see.
Jianfeng.

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:35.890
- You know the neural space is just like your brain.

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:40.270
You can come up with an answer to a question.

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:44.300
I really don't know how you
generate the answer in your brain.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:47.920
The brain is, in the neural network,
like a neural space.

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:55.140
So usually you take the question
from the symbolic space and map it in the neural space.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:58.010
Now in the neural space,
that's where all these vectors are.

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:00.620
You generate an answer, in your brain.

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:06.500
Then you need to speak out
this generates the answer,

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:10.390
and then by transporting it
from the neural space

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:11.630
to the symbolic space,

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:13.300
so that people can understand it.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:16.310
- I see.
So you're getting some real human input.

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:19.650
You put it through this washing machine
and this stuff happens there.

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:22.160
And then what it does is it goes backwards
out of the washing machine

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:24.150
and then it outputs a really, really good answer.

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.740
- Yes.
- So what are some real-world applications

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:28.820
of your research
that people are actually using?

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:32.270
- So one of the areas that we were looking at is,

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:35.960
how do we take the state-of-the-art

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:37.900
models in machine
reading comprehension,

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:39.860
and how do we put it to use
with something

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:41.430
that would actually help people?

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:44.840
And the first thing, and the thing
that came to my mind was, you know in your car,

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:47.180
you have that
400 page auto manual

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:49.390
and you probably have
never read it?

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:53.000
Yet, if your car gets in trouble,
all that information is there.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:57.680
So the idea was, can we ingest large
documents like a help manual,

00:11:57.680 --> 00:11:59.640
user guides, et cetera, and then be able to do

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:01.370
Q&amp;A on this document?

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:05.570
- I see.
So next time I can't make my TV work,

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:08.220
I just feel the manual into the computer
and I ask the computer,

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:10.350
hey, how do I make this part of my TV work?

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:10.730
- Yes.

00:12:10.730 --> 00:12:14.670
Or where is this in the TV
or why is this not happening?

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:15.980
Exactly.
- I see.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:19.230
And is this a piece of software
that's out in production somewhere?

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:20.370
- It's in development.

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:21.760
- OK, I see.
I see.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:23.870
So this is really cool.

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:25.490
What's next?

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:30.950
- I think, you know, the big area as I mentioned,
a couple areas was knowledge representation.

00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:35.970
So while we've made strides in reasoning,
how do we represent,

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:39.650
how do we factor reasoning
from knowledge representation?

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:42.820
So that we can give it
any arbitrary amount of knowledge,

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:44.310
and the system can
then reason on that.

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:48.620
And then the other area that I think
is really key, is common sense reasoning.

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:53.470
Which is, you know, that people haven't,
people are working on it,

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:56.350
but we haven't really made
any major breakthroughs in this.

00:12:56.350 --> 00:12:57.940
- How do you do that?
- The difficulty with this,

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:01.050
I would say the difficulty is that we are often working

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:03.620
in what we call a single modality
Whereas humans,

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:06.540
we experience everything
in many, many modalities.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:11.060
We have sight, we have hearing,
we have text, we have all sorts of stuff

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:13.100
and we just we get common sense,

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:15.470
you and I, by experiencing it in the world.

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:19.920
But when we give it to this computer,
the computer can't experience the world.

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:24.060
And you know, text sometimes
is not only enough to learn.

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:25.830
And that's what makes it really, really difficult.

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:26.460
- I see.

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:30.450
So there's like a loss in fidelity
in just having things in text.

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:33.170
- Yes.
Because, and this is

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:35.290
because common sense is assumed, right?

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:39.550
When we write text, we assume through
the experience out in the world

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:41.230
that the common sense is already there,

